---
// /what-it-runs-on — the technology behind a Croftweaver site, in plain words. Positioning term
// (tests/positioning.spec.ts): "what it runs on", in the title, the description and the H1; "tech
// stack" in the body. It builds the 5 October 2026 /stack spec (origin/docs/teams-page-spec) at this
// address and adds Astro, the assistant, the checks and where a shop fits. Plan, review trail and the
// source of every claim: the private hub's what-it-runs-on/ (PAGE-PLAN.md, REVIEW-plan-2026-10-22.md).
// Every vendor figure is dated READ and linked; read them again before changing the page.
// The page claims no ranking, no price a vendor does not state, no endorsement by a vendor, and
// never "free" without its limits.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

const READ = '22 October 2026';
const PRESS = 'https://www.cloudflare.com/press-releases/2026/cloudflare-acquires-astro-to-accelerate-the-future-of-high-performance-web-development/';
const RUNS = `${SITE.repo}/blob/main/docs/WHAT-THE-SKILLS-RUN.md`;
const START = `${SITE.repo}/blob/main/docs/GETTING-STARTED.md`;
const AGENTS = `${SITE.repo}/blob/main/skills/new-website/templates/AGENTS.md`;
const DEPLOY = `${SITE.repo}/blob/main/skills/new-website/references/CLOUDFLARE_FIRST_DEPLOY.md`;
---
<Base
  title="What it runs on: Astro, Cloudflare, GitHub"
  description="What it runs on: Astro builds the pages, GitHub keeps the files, Cloudflare serves them. The tech stack of a Croftweaver site, what it costs, what you can swap."
  image="/images/og/what-it-runs-on.jpg"
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>What it runs on: three places, all in your name</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The tech stack of a Croftweaver site is three places: a folder on your computer,
      your files on GitHub, and Cloudflare, which builds the site and serves it. Each one is
      yours. Your AI assistant works in the first place. Astro, the framework that turns your
      text files into pages, runs there and runs again at Cloudflare. This page says what each
      part does, what it costs and what you can swap.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
      <a class="btn" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="picture">
    <h2>The picture</h2>
    <ul class="places" role="list">
      <li class="place" data-place>
        <h3><a href="#folder">Your computer</a></h3>
        <p>The folder with your site's files. Your assistant works here. Astro builds a preview, and the tests run here.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="place" data-place>
        <h3><a href="#github">GitHub</a></h3>
        <p>Your files and the history of every change. The checks run here again on every pull request.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="place" data-place>
        <h3><a href="#cloudflare">Cloudflare</a></h3>
        <p>Builds the site from GitHub and serves it. It can also hold your domain's records.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">Your computer and GitHub exchange changes. Cloudflare builds from GitHub.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="astro">
    <h2>Astro builds the pages</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> is an open-source framework for content websites.
      It turns your text files and templates into plain HTML pages when the site is built. It
      runs on your computer, to build the preview and the site the tests check, and again at Cloudflare each time
      Cloudflare builds the site from GitHub. A direct upload, which the toolkit also describes,
      builds on your computer only.
    </p>
    <p>
      Astro makes static pages by default. It can build a page on request instead, with a small
      add-on for the host (an adapter); its own documentation on
      <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/on-demand-rendering/">on-demand rendering</a>
      says how (read {READ}). Croftweaver sites are static: no cart, no accounts, no database.
      The files Astro writes are ordinary HTML, and any web host can serve them.
    </p>
    <p>
      In January 2026, Cloudflare <a href={PRESS}>announced</a> that the Astro team is joining it
      and that Astro will remain open source (read {READ}). Croftweaver is an independent project.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="assistant">
    <h2>Your assistant does the typing</h2>
    <p>
      An AI coding assistant edits the files, runs the tests and, when you ask, publishes the
      site. Croftweaver is developed and used with Claude Code. Codex and Antigravity have each
      been tested from start to finish on one site (the maintainer's word, 18 October 2026; there
      is no written record yet). There is a plugin for Claude Code and for Codex.
    </p>
    <p>
      You choose the steps, as <a href="/why#steps">the four steps on the why page</a> say. In
      Claude Code, routine build commands, <code>git push</code> among them, run without asking.
      That saves clicks and is not a safety fence. The <code>AGENTS.md</code> in a site tells the assistant
      never to run <code>npm run ship</code> unasked, but that is a written rule, not something Claude Code
      enforces. <a href={RUNS}>What the skills run</a> lists the commands. The list of what runs without
      asking was read from Claude Code's documentation and not tried in a live session.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="folder">
    <h2>A folder on your computer</h2>
    <p>
      This is the working copy: a folder that mirrors the repository on GitHub. Your assistant
      reads and changes the files there and runs the checks before anything leaves your computer.
      Each person who works on the site has their own folder. GitHub is where the changes meet.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Keep that folder out of anything that syncs files.</strong> Git's own FAQ advises
      against using a cloud syncing service for any part of a Git repository, because it can cause
      corruption (<a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/gitfaq#sync-working-tree">the git FAQ</a>, read
      {READ}). It names no product. iCloud Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox and Google Drive are examples of
      such services. The toolkit's <a href={START}>getting-started guide</a> says to pick a folder
      outside a Documents folder that syncs to iCloud or OneDrive.
    </p>
    <p>
      A folder on a shared server or network drive is a different problem: two people working in
      one folder overwrite each other, and GitHub is already where a team shares.
    </p>
    <p>
      Codex in the browser needs no folder. Each task starts from a fresh copy of your repository
      on GitHub. An older task that you resume may be behind, as the instructions for assistants
      that the toolkit gives a new site (<code>AGENTS.md</code>, <a href={AGENTS}>the template</a>) warn.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="github">
    <h2>GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      GitHub keeps your site's files and the history of every change, and it runs the checks on each
      pull request. The account is yours, and the repository can be private or public. A free account
      allows <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/githubs-plans">unlimited
      public and private repositories</a> (read {READ}).
    </p>
    <p>
      The checks run as GitHub Actions. They are
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/billing/concepts/product-billing/github-actions">free on public
      repositories</a> with GitHub's standard runners. Private repositories on the free plan get 2,000
      minutes a month (read {READ}).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="cloudflare">
    <h2>Cloudflare</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare builds the site from GitHub and serves it. Every pull request from a branch in your
      repository gets its own preview address; a pull request from a fork gets none
      (<a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/preview-deployments/">Cloudflare's preview deployments, read {READ}</a>).
    </p>
    <h3>What the free plan covers</h3>
    <p>
      The free plan covers a small static site. For Pages it allows 500 builds a month, one build at a
      time, 20,000 files per site, 25 MiB per file and 100 custom domains per project
      (<a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/platform/limits/">Cloudflare's limits, read {READ}</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      One small function runs on every request to a Croftweaver site. It keeps preview copies out of
      search engines and sends the <code>pages.dev</code> address to your own domain, and in the optional
      server mode for Plausible it also counts visits. Cloudflare says that
      once a Pages project has a function,
      <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/functions/routing/">all requests invoke it by default</a>.
      So the free plan's limit of 100,000 requests a day
      (<a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/">Cloudflare's Workers limits, read {READ}</a>),
      which <a href={DEPLOY}>the toolkit's guide</a> says every Function and Worker in your account shares, counts the whole
      site, not only pages that need a server. When the limit is used up, a project setting decides: serve
      the pages without the function, or show an error page. The toolkit tells you to set it to serve the
      pages, so the pages keep being served and the function's jobs pause until the limit resets.
    </p>
    <h3>Your domain</h3>
    <p>
      Your domain's DNS can move to Cloudflare; the full setup is available on every plan
      (<a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/dns/zone-setups/full-setup/">Cloudflare's DNS setup, read {READ}</a>). You change the name servers at the
      company where you bought the domain, and the domain stays registered there: Cloudflare's
      <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/dns/faq/">DNS FAQ</a> says you do not need to move away
      from your registrar (read {READ}). The move has two traps above all: mail records, and DNSSEC, which
      has to be switched off at the old registrar first or the domain stops resolving.
      <a href={DEPLOY}>A checklist in the toolkit</a> walks you through them before anything is switched.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the company that registered your domain forwards your mail today, check before the switch
      whether the forwarding keeps working once the DNS is elsewhere. With some companies it stops,
      because it depends on their own DNS.
      <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/email-service/get-started/route-emails/">Cloudflare Email
      Routing</a> (read {READ}) is one replacement: it forwards incoming mail to a mailbox you already have, it is
      available on every plan, and it needs your DNS at Cloudflare. It is not a mailbox of its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloudflare now recommends Workers for new projects. The toolkit deploys to Pages, which
      Cloudflare still offers on every plan
      (<a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/">Cloudflare's Pages page, read {READ}</a>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="keystatic">
    <h2>Where Keystatic fits</h2>
    <p>
      Not as a fourth place. <a href="https://keystatic.com">Keystatic</a> is an optional editing screen
      for someone who would rather fill in a form than talk to an assistant, chosen when the site is first
      built. In local mode, the one the toolkit sets up, you open the editing screen in your browser on
      your own computer. It writes text files (Markdoc, a Markdown dialect) into the same folder, and you send the change
      to GitHub as usual. The published site contains no Keystatic.
    </p>
    <p>
      A GitHub mode serves the editing screen from the published site and saves straight to GitHub, so
      nothing runs on the editor's computer. It needs a server part on Cloudflare, a GitHub App and stored
      secrets. The toolkit describes it but does not set it up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="checks">
    <h2>Checks before anything goes live</h2>
    <p>
      A hook builds the site and runs the test suites before every push. GitHub runs them again on every
      pull request and on pushes to <code>main</code> and <code>production</code>. In the recommended
      two-stage setup, a change appears first on a preview address that search engines are told to
      ignore, and one command, <code>npm run ship</code>, publishes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What enforces what.</strong> The hook can be skipped. The checks on GitHub report a
      result; they block nothing by themselves. Whether GitHub also refuses a merge until a check passes
      depends on a branch rule that the owner switches on. The toolkit's team setup describes it and says a
      free plan allows it only for a public repository. On
      the repository behind croftweaver.com it is on: a change reaches <code>main</code> only by a pull request that
      passes the <code>test</code> and <code>Cloudflare Pages</code> checks, and the rule has no bypass
      list (read from GitHub's settings on {READ}). GitHub requires no passing check for the push that
      publishes to <code>production</code>; only the hook runs, unless it is skipped.
      <a href="/checks">What each suite checks, and what a pass does not prove</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="costs">
    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      It is free to start: GitHub's free plan and Cloudflare's free plan, each with the limits above, as
      read on {READ}. Croftweaver itself is MIT-licensed and charges nothing. Free here means free within
      those limits, and plans change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not free: your domain, at the price of whoever registers it; your AI assistant's own plan; and
      Plausible, if you switch it on. Plausible counts visits without cookies
      (<a href="https://plausible.io/data-policy">its data policy</a>). Its
      <a href="https://plausible.io/#pricing">pricing page</a> showed $9 for up to 10,000 page views
      on {READ} and did not say whether that is billed monthly or yearly. The
      <a href="/start#analytics">quickstart</a> gives the Starter plan as 9 euros or US dollars a month, depending on where you are.
      You can also host Plausible yourself
      (<a href="https://github.com/plausible/community-edition">the community edition</a>). Plausible stays
      off until you switch it on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="swap">
    <h2>What you can swap</h2>
    <p>
      The pages are static files, and any web host can serve them. The setup around them is written for
      Cloudflare Pages: the publish script, the previews, the headers file, the small function and, if you
      add it, Keystatic's GitHub mode. Another host means replacing those parts or doing without them.
    </p>
    <h3 id="shop">Where a shop fits</h3>
    <p>
      A shop stays on its own platform, such as Shopify. Croftweaver builds the content next to it: guides,
      articles and landing pages that link to your products. It has no Shopify connection, and a cart,
      accounts or checkout need a server part that Croftweaver does not build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="next">
    <h2>Next step</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/start">quickstart</a> starts from nothing, and <a href="/checks">the test gate</a> lists
      every suite. The code is on <a href={SITE.repo}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
      <a class="btn" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The three places: side by side from 48rem, one under the other below it. A long word stays inside the
     screen if the box can shrink: overflow-wrap: anywhere on the box lets it, and so does minmax(0, 1fr) on
     the column together with the site's overflow-wrap: break-word. Either is enough and the page has both
     (measured at 360 and 320 px). tests/a11y.spec.ts puts a long word in a box and fails when neither is left. */
  .places { list-style: none; margin: 1rem 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .place { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem; box-shadow: var(--shadow); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .place h3 { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .place p { margin: 0; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) { .places { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
</style>
